<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🍂 LIVE LEAF COLOR</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<h2>LIVE LEAF COLOR</h2><p><b>Data time:</b> VI composite start 2026-08-05 (+16d window); reflectance 2026-08-13 (+8d); retrieved 2026-09-25 05:25 PM EDT<br/><b>Source updated:</b> composite sig 1ce56c63a4cc<br/><b>Source version:</b> leaf-v7-1ce56c63a4cc<br/><b>Processed:</b> 2026-09-25 05:25 PM EDT<br/><b>Status:</b> LATEST AVAILABLE COMPOSITE</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/leaf_color/legend.png?v=leaf-v7-1ce56c63a4cc" width="600" alt="legend"><br>What each color means (one trip around the year, left to right on the key): <b>deep blue</b> = dormant winter; <b>light blue/cyan</b> = spring awakening and bud break; <b>teal-green</b> = leaf emergence; <b>green</b> = growing season; <b>yellow-green/yellow</b> = first color; <b>orange</b> = autumn coloring; <b>red/magenta</b> = peak color into leaf drop; <b>purple</b> = late drop returning to <b>deep-blue</b> dormant (the scale wraps: both ends are winter). Color is the satellite-derived growth stage, not the color of individual trees. Only land the satellite actually saw this composite is painted — <b>transparent = clouds, snow, cities, bare ground, water, or otherwise no current observation</b>.</p><p><b>Source:</b> NASA MODIS Aqua MYD13A1/MYD09A1 via Planetary Computer (global, 500 m)<br/><a href="https://planetarycomputer.microsoft.com/">https://planetarycomputer.microsoft.com/</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <GroundOverlay>
      <name>🍂 LIVE LEAF COLOR</name>
      <drawOrder>10</drawOrder>
      <Icon>
